            <ArticleTitle>Laparoscopic transgastric resection of a large pedunculated gastric lipoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1136/bcr-2016-215791</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">bcr2016215791</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Large gastric lipomas are a rare condition. They are often asymptomatic but may present with upper gastrointestinal bleeding or gastric outlet obstruction. We describe the case of a 52-year-old woman with a large gastric lipoma presenting with pain due to intermittent prolapse through the pylorus. The patient was treated using a novel approach of laparoscopic transgastric resection and was discharged on postoperative day 3. She suffered no complication and her histology confirmed a gastric lipoma with clear surgical margins.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>2016 BMJ Publishing Group Ltd.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
